        <body>
            <div xml:id="d429">

                <pb facs="0689" n="573" sameAs="frus:frus1923v01/pg573"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.51/4450</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the British Chargé</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Chilton</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1923-12-18"><hi rendition="#italic">December 18,
                        1923</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the receipt
                    of your note No. 1061 of December 12, 1923, relating to the situation at Canton,
                    China, and the apparent success thus far of the joint naval demonstration. With
                    reference to the action which is being taken by this Government on behalf of the
                    integrity of the Customs, I am happy to inform you that, on December 5,
                    instructions were issued for a concentration at Canton of the available American
                    naval units; and that, on December 15, further instructions directed the
                    Commander of the South China Patrol to cooperate with the naval commanders of
                    the other foreign Powers in the plan which had been approved by the Consular
                    Body at Canton for preventing the seizure of the Canton Customs House.</p>
                <p>Accept [etc.]</p>
                <closer>For the Secretary of State:<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">William
                            Phillips</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
